EXT. SALT FLAT TELEGRAPH OFFICE - MIDDAY

White ground. White sky. One shack. A wire running east into nothing.

ISAIAH CROW (40s, duster, hat low) steps off a dust-caked horse. Ties it. Slow. Looks at the door.

Shut.

He knocks.

INT. TELEGRAPH OFFICE - CONTINUOUS

Dim. A key chattering on a desk.

MAUDE PELLETIER (50s, sleeves rolled, ink on her fingers) taps without looking up.

The door opens. Light cuts across the floor.

ISAIAH
Afternoon.

MAUDE
Afternoon.

She keeps tapping.

ISAIAH
Got a wire to send.

MAUDE
Paper's on the left.

He takes a sheet. Writes. Folds it. Sets it down.

She reads without picking it up.

MAUDE (CONT'D)
Two words.

ISAIAH
It's all I need.

MAUDE
Ninety cents.

He counts coins onto the desk. She doesn't count them.

MAUDE (CONT'D)
Coffee?

ISAIAH
If it's made.

She pours from a pot on a cold stove. Sets a tin cup near his hand. Her other hand stays under the desk.

ISAIAH (CONT'D)
Your husband about?

MAUDE
Out at the well.

ISAIAH
Long walk.

MAUDE
He likes it.

He lifts the cup. Doesn't drink. Sets it down.

ISAIAH
Funny. Well's dry since August.

The key stops chattering.

Maude's shoulders drop an inch.

MAUDE
Then I suppose he won't be back.

A pause. The wire hums.

Isaiah turns the paper toward her. Two words in pencil: SHE KNOWS.

ISAIAH
I'd say that's still true.

Maude draws a revolver from beneath the desk. Lays it flat on the coins.

MAUDE
Ninety cents. Sheriff.
